  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Monday...\n\nSeasonably cool conditions exist today as temperatures warm into \nthe 40s and 50s across the region. While dry conditions prevail \nfor most, a few light rain and snow showers (snow levels around \n6,000 to 8,000 feet) exist over far southern Montana and northern \nWyoming within an area of diffluence from a low pressure system to\nthe south. As this low pressure system gradually shifts east \nthrough Utah and Colorado today and Wednesday, the associated \nshowers will gradually shift eastward as well. By Wednesday \nafternoon and evening, the chance of precipitation is over the \nplains of far south-eastern Montana and north-eastern Wyoming \nbefore a dry cold front passage ends the chance of precipitation \nacross the region Wednesday night. \n\nOutside of the precipitation, fog is forecast to develop over \nportions of the area tonight. With this, locally dense fog is \npossible at times. Make sure to use caution if traveling tonight \nor early Wednesday morning as visibility could change rapidly. \n\nAfter a warmer day on Wednesday (temperatures back near normal), \nthe previously mentioned cold front will bring slightly cooler \ntemperatures back to the area on Thursday. This front will also \nincrease winds, especially over the plains of far eastern Montana \nwhere peak wind gusts are forecast in the 40s to 50 mph on \nThursday. Across the rest of the area, gusts in the teens to 20s \nmph are forecast.\n\nFriday into the weekend, a ridge of high pressure is expected to \nbuild over the region. This will allow temperatures to climb back \nabove normal into the 60s and 70s, warmest Saturday. Along with \nthe warmer temperatures, windy conditions are forecast at times. \nThe strongest winds are expected around Livingston to Nye late \nFriday into Saturday ahead of a dry cold front passage late \nSaturday night into Sunday morning. \n\nMonday looks to be mainly dry and warm, but ensembles suggest \nanother weather system is possible by Tuesday or Wednesday next \nweek. LA",
